    <article id="incident-001" class="incident-card">
      <div class="incident-meta">
        <a href="#day-03" class="incident-day-badge" title="View Day 03 in Timeline">DAY 03</a>
        <span>INCIDENT #001</span>
        <span>LOW</span>
        <span>ACCESS / COMPUTE</span>
      </div>

      <h2>EC2 SSH Authentication Failure</h2>

      <div class="incident-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Symptom</h3>
          <pre><code>ssh ubuntu@&lt;host&gt;

Permission denied (publickey)</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Investigation</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>EC2 instance was verified in running state in application subnet</li>
            <li>Security group allowed inbound TCP/22 from administrator IP</li>
            <li>Public IPv4 address was attached and responding to network probes</li>
            <li>Terraform state inspection showed <code>key_name = null</code></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="root-cause">
        <span>ROOT CAUSE</span>
        <p>
          The EC2 instance was provisioned without an EC2 key pair because the <code>key_name</code> parameter was omitted in the Terraform compute configuration.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="remediation">
        <h3>Remediation</h3>
        <pre><code>key_name = var.key_name</code></pre>
        <p>
          Updated the Terraform configuration to explicitly associate the instance with the authorized SSH key pair. Applied changes and confirmed immediate successful authentication.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="validation-box">
        <h3>Validation & Engineering Lesson</h3>
        <p>
          Validated SSH connection via key pair. Established a core engineering guideline: all compute definitions in IaC must enforce explicit key pair assignment before initiating software provisioning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Incident #002 -->
    <article id="incident-002" class="incident-card">
      <div class="incident-meta">
        <a href="#day-06" class="incident-day-badge" title="View Day 06 in Timeline">DAY 06</a>
        <span>INCIDENT #002</span>
        <span>HIGH</span>
        <span>AUTO SCALING / RECOVERY TEST</span>
      </div>

      <h2>Instance Replacement Test (Controlled Termination)</h2>

      <div class="incident-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Scenario</h3>
          <p>
            An active application EC2 instance in us-east-1a was deliberately terminated via AWS CLI to test Auto Scaling Group self-healing and zero-downtime recovery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Expected Behavior</h3>
          <p>
            The Auto Scaling Group detects capacity loss via ELB health check, terminates the degraded node, provisions a replacement instance from the Launch Template, and registers it to the Target Group.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="root-cause">
        <span>OBSERVED BEHAVIOR</span>
        <pre><code>Instance Terminated (us-east-1a)
       ↓
ASG detected capacity deficit (1/2 desired)
       ↓
Scaling activity initiated replacement launch
       ↓
Cloud-init executed user-data bootstrap
       ↓
Target registered to ALB Target Group
       ↓
Health checks passed HTTP 200 OK (2/2 capacity restored)</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="remediation">
        <h3>Validation</h3>
        <p>
          Target Group reached full healthy state (2/2 instances) within evaluation threshold. Public ALB endpoint maintained 100% uptime with zero client 5xx errors during node replacement.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="validation-box">
        <h3>Engineering Lesson</h3>
        <p>
          Stateless compute paired with immutable Launch Templates and health-aware Auto Scaling Groups ensures automated self-healing without requiring human intervention during infrastructure failures.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>


