  <!-- Technology Decisions Section -->
  <section id="decisions" class="decisions-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span>TECHNOLOGY DECISIONS</span>
      <span class="heading-badge">WHY THESE COMPONENTS?</span>
    </div>

    <div class="decision-grid">
      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">NETWORK ISOLATION</span>
        <h2>VPC & Subnet Design</h2>
        <p>
          Provides private network boundaries with explicit tiering. Public application subnets receive ingress from the load balancer, while private database subnets have no internet gateway route, guaranteeing databases are unreachable from the public internet.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">TRAFFIC DISTRIBUTION</span>
        <h2>Application Load Balancer</h2>
        <p>
          Distributes Layer 7 HTTP traffic evenly across multiple Availability Zones. Continuously probes backend nodes against the <code>/health</code> contract, automatically routing requests away from degraded instances.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">DEFENSE IN DEPTH</span>
        <h2>Security Groups</h2>
        <p>
          Enforces least-privilege traffic flow through security group referencing. The ALB allows public HTTP/80, while EC2 security groups permit HTTP ingress exclusively from the ALB security group ID.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">IMMUTABLE COMPUTE</span>
        <h2>Launch Template</h2>
        <p>
          Codifies instance configuration, dynamic Ubuntu LTS AMI resolution via <code>data.aws_ami</code>, instance sizing, and automated cloud-init bootstrap, ensuring all compute capacity is created consistently.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">SELF-HEALING ARCHITECTURE</span>
        <h2>Auto Scaling Group</h2>
        <p>
          Maintains desired compute capacity across two Availability Zones. Integrated with ELB health checks to automatically terminate degraded instances and launch healthy replacements without manual intervention.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">ISOLATED PERSISTENCE</span>
        <h2>RDS MariaDB</h2>
        <p>
          Managed relational database deployed across private subnets with automated snapshots, KMS encryption at rest, and port 3306 restricted solely to the application security group.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">ACTIONABLE TELEMETRY</span>
        <h2>CloudWatch & Observability</h2>
        <p>
          Combines Unified Agent OS metrics with ALB target response time and error rate alarms, providing high-resolution visibility and automated alerts on service degradation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">RUNTIME ISOLATION</span>
        <h2>Node Version Manager (NVM)</h2>
        <p>
          Installs and manages Node.js LTS in non-root user space (<code>/home/ubuntu/.nvm</code>), preventing system package pollution and ensuring predictable, isolated application runtimes across deployments.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="decision-card">
        <span class="eyebrow">WEB TIER</span>
        <h2>Nginx Reverse Proxy</h2>
        <p>
          Acts as a high-performance reverse proxy listening on port 80 and forwarding requests to the Node.js application process on port 3000, managing header sanitization, connection buffering, and host routing.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


